  ],
  "text": "About eighty percent of the ocean floor has never been directly observed. Meanwhile there is a camera on Mars taking photographs right now. From orbit it is easy. Point a lens down and the surface is just there. Point that same lens at the sea and you get ten metres of water, then nothing. Radar is no better. Water simply swallows it. So the only way left is sonar, and sonar means sailing over every square kilometre. Nobody has done that. Doing it would take roughly two hundred ship years. Which is the real reason a new species turns up in the news every few weeks. We are not running out of planet to explore. We just cannot see through water. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
